<!--
     Aperture video-configuration overlay for oppo sdm660 (R11 / R11s).

     Resource names and types must match the app keys one-for-one, because
     Aperture looks them up by entry name + type in the overlay package
     (OverlaysRepository.kt:22-31, 133-143).

     How a video grade reaches Aperture (the three sources are independent):

     1. Quality (SD/HD/FHD/UHD) - CameraX `Recorder.getVideoCapabilities()` with its
        default `VIDEO_CAPABILITIES_SOURCE_CAMCORDER_PROFILE`, i.e. the legacy
        `CamcorderProfile` / `EncoderProfiles` surface, i.e. `configs/media/
        media_profiles_vendor.xml` for this product (not the HAL stream sizes!). A
        `2160p` profile is what makes a UHD chip appear; without one CameraX tops out
        at FHD. This overlay cannot add a quality (it only carries frame rates for
        qualities that already exist) - the lever for a quality is that XML, and it is
        also a hard constraint: a `qhd` (1440p) profile makes CameraX report
        `Quality.QHD`, which Aperture does not map and rejects with "Unknown video
        quality" when it renders or cycles the quality button. This tree disables
        qhd/2k/4kdci/2160p for camera ids 2/3 (the aux/paired devices the RRO exposes)
        for exactly that reason.
     2. Frame rate (24/30/60/120) - CameraX `CameraInfo.supportedFrameRateRanges`
        = `android.control.aeAvailableTargetFpsRanges`, filtered through
        `FrameRate.fromRange()` (a chip needs a *fixed* range: [n,n]) and then
        added/removed by this array.
        That list is now derived in the HAL from the device's decoded capability
        (`QCamera2/HAL3/QCamera3VideoGrades.h`): the vendor pairs verbatim, the
        fixed endpoints of every pair, and [60,60] when a 60 fps HFR sensor tier
        at 1080p or larger exists. `QCamera3HWI.cpp` accepts a request whose range
        is one of those pairs or a sub-range of one.
        Consequence for this overlay: an *addition* would be both unnecessary and
        unsafe - the decisive fact (does this unit's fps table really carry 60 fps,
        and at which size?) is only known on the device, and a chip whose range the
        HAL refuses breaks the recording session instead of degrading. The HAL
        publishes exactly what the sensor declares, so nothing here has to guess.
        This array therefore only *removes* grades.
     3. Dynamic range (SDR/HLG10/HDR10) - no 10-bit camera record exists on this
        platform (camera/docs/DYNAMIC_RANGE.md), so SDR stays the only value and
        nothing is claimed here.

     Encoder envelope behind the removals:
       - `configs/media/media_codecs_vendor.xml` AVC :150-161 / HEVC :134-146 declare
         `max 3840x2160` and `blocks-per-second max 972000`; those two limits are
         identical in the stock R11s file, i.e. they are the real R11/R11s envelope.
         972000 = ceil(3840/16) * ceil(2160/16) * 30, exactly 4K30; 4K60 would need
         1944000 and 1080p120 needs 979200, so both are removed where they apply.
         (The `performance-point-3840x2160` limits in the shipped file are NOT stock
         R11/R11s: `grep -c performance-point stock/R11s/vendor/etc/media_codecs_vendor.xml`
         is 0, they exist only in the PAAM00 variant this tree copies. The R11/R11s
         measured row is `configs/media/media_codecs_performance.xml` HEVC
         `measured-frame-rate-3840x2160 range="6-24"`.)
       - No R11/R11s sensor declares a 3840x2160 mode (the imx398 mode table has
         4096x2160 and 4608x3456, all <=30 fps), and CameraX maps Quality.UHD to
         exactly 3840x2160, so the UHD removals below are expected to be no-ops on
         this hardware; they stay as a fail-closed guard.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Keep high-resolution output sizes enabled. This is already the app
         default (Aperture res/values/config.xml:86); the entry is explicit so a
         future change of the default cannot silently downgrade still capture. -->
    <bool name="config_enableHighResolution">true</bool>

    <!-- Triplets: (camera id, qualities, frame rates). A '-' prefix removes a
         rate for those qualities (OverlaysRepository.kt:75-79).
         Only removals, never additions - see the header for why:
           - sd/hd/fhd @ 120 fps: 1080p120 needs 979200 blocks/s > 972000, and
             Aperture never opens a constrained-high-speed session, which is the
             only session type this HAL admits >=120 fps in.
           - uhd @ 60/120 fps: above the same ceiling. Expected to be a no-op: none
             of the camera ids this tree exposes has a 2160p profile any more, so
             there is no UHD quality to attach a rate to. Kept as a fail-closed guard
             in case a profile is added later.
         60 fps is deliberately not touched: after the HAL derivation it appears
         exactly when the decoded table (or a 1080p+ 60 fps HFR tier) supports it
         and disappears otherwise, so nothing here can make a wrong chip appear.
         Ids 3/4 (paired / logical) carry the same guards as 0/1/2 so a future
         publication of those devices cannot expose an unencodable grade. -->
    <string-array name="config_additionalVideoConfigurations">
        <!-- Back main camera. -->
        <item>0</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60|-120</item>
        <item>0</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>-120</item>
        <!-- Front camera. -->
        <item>1</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60|-120</item>
        <item>1</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>-120</item>
        <!-- Back auxiliary camera. -->
        <item>2</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60|-120</item>
        <item>2</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>-120</item>
        <!-- Paired device (published only when the pair succeeds). -->
        <item>3</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60|-120</item>
        <item>3</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>-120</item>
        <!-- Logical device on top of the pair. -->
        <item>4</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60|-120</item>
        <item>4</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>-120</item>
    </string-array>

    <!--
    Multi-camera. Aperture always keeps ids 0 and 1 and drops everything else unless
    config_enableAuxCameras is true (CameraRepository.kt:119,151 and :128), and it
    additionally drops every camera whose physicalCameraInfos.size > 1 unless
    config_ignoreLogicalAuxCameras is false (CameraRepository.kt:125-126) - that is
    exactly the logical device QCamera2Factory publishes (public id 4 = backend
    paired device, physical ids 0 and 2). Both switches are therefore required for
    the multi-lens surface to exist at all on this unit. To revert: false/true.
    -->
    <bool name="config_enableAuxCameras">true</bool>
    <bool name="config_ignoreLogicalAuxCameras">false</bool>

    <!-- No id is ignored: 0/1 main, 2 standalone rear aux, 3 paired device (published
         only when the paired publication succeeds), 4 logical (published on top of 3).
         Add "3" only after a device read-back shows it duplicates the pair in
         Aperture's lens selector. -->
    <string-array name="config_ignoredAuxCameraIds" />

    <!-- Not needed on this port and therefore absent: CameraX drops any camera whose
         characteristics lack REQUEST_AVAILABLE_CAPABILITIES_BACKWARD_COMPATIBLE
         unless it is listed here, and `applyOppoBasicMetadataPolicy()` adds that
         capability unconditionally (QCamera3HWI.cpp), so ids 2/3/4 are admitted
         without an exemption. Add an id here only if a future change makes the
         capability conditional. -->

    <!-- Deliberately empty. Aperture's lens selector only uses these entries when
         at least one ratio pair is present (LensSelectorLayout.kt:55): a pair makes
         the selector call smoothZoom(exactZoomRatio) on that camera instead of
         switching device, and every label claims a real optical ratio. No
         defect-free value is available for this unit from the tree or from the
         stock camera app (the ColorOS app ships a continuous zoom seek bar -
         "Zoom" / "變焦" - not a per-lens button), so nothing is invented here. The
         raw per-unit numbers to read back before filling this in: the logical
         camera's ZOOM_RATIO_RANGE and its physical cameras'
         LENS_INFO_AVAILABLE_FOCAL_LENGTHS. -->
    <string-array name="config_logicalZoomRatios" />
</resources>
